For `llvm.masked.scatter`, overlapping addresses are guaranteed to be ordered from the least-significant vector element to the most-significant vector element—i.e., lane 0, then lane 1, and so on.

Thus, when multiple active lanes target the same address, the highest-numbered active lane’s value remains in memory. This is equivalent to serial scalar stores:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
  if (mask[i])
    base[i] = value[i];
```

LLVM’s implementation must preserve this ordering even when splitting a scatter into multiple operations; the lower-element operation must be chained before the higher-element operation.
